WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.600
With the release of this fall's Joker starring
Joaquin Phoenix, we'll see the debut of the

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.980
latest theatrical take on one of the most
iconic comic book supervillains ever created.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.080
But believe it or not it turns out that Phoenix
could've been cast in the role of a completely

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.280
different legendary bad guy...not for DC,
but for Marvel.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.980
That's the word according to Charlie Wen,
former head of visual development for Marvel

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.400
Studios, and the guy who led the team in charge
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe's whole visual

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:24.900
design.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.660
You may have heard that Phoenix famously turned
down the role of Doctor Strange back in 2014

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.279
— a part that eventually went to Benedict
Cumberbatch.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.449
But before that, apparently he was also one
of the Hollywood A-listers to audition for

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.560
the role of the Red Skull in 2011's Captain
America: The First Avenger.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.520
According to Wen, who tells us that he had
created a couple versions of Phoenix in the

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.160
role, the actor, quote, "definitely could
have added a quirky/creepy feel to Red Skull."

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:51.920
If you're wondering about just what Phoenix
as the Red Skull would've looked like at least

00:00:51.929 --> 00:00:55.870
while the comic book villain still wore his
human skin look no further than the exclusive

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.920
concept art Wen provided to Looper.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.060
One thing that stands out in this image immediately
is Phoenix's youth.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.440
About 14 years younger than Hugo Weaving,
Phoenix as the Red Skull would've been much

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.160
closer in age to Chris Evans' Captain America.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.979
Would a closer age gap have made a difference
to how it felt to watch Captain America beat

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:12.760
up the Red Skull?

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.320
Does the Red Skull need to be older and have
a claim on more wisdom and experience than

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.380
Cap to be more convincingly threatening?

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.120
Maybe!

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.380
Then again...maybe not!

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.280
But more important than all that, it's not
hard to see that Wen captured Phoenix's quiet

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.600
intensity pretty easily in his mock-up.

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:30.660
The look in his eyes clearly communicates
the kind of authoritarian lunacy that can

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.260
only radiate off of the leader of HYDRA.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:33.880
"Hail Hydra."

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.040
"HAIL HYDRA!"

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.140
It's really fun to imagine how Phoenix's hyper-focused
take on the Red Skull might've affected the

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.020
overall film.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.820
However, Wen points out that such an intense
villain might not have actually served the

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:47.200
film very well as a whole.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.580
"I think he would probably have stood out
more than was called for in that role.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.979
[...] Hugo [Weaving], on the other hand, brought
out a Red Skull that was pretty much just

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:58.590
right for the story at the time and for where
Marvel was in that point in the MCU, one that

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.840
isn't making too large of a statement for
the role."

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.360
That makes sense, when you think about it.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.900
Captain America: The First Avenger is a period
piece, an origin story, a family-friendly

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:10.870
war movie, an adventure film, and a set-up
for one of the biggest crossover films the

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.030
Avengers ever made up to that point.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.220
Weaving's turn as a coolly evil supervillain
slots in perfectly, providing just enough

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.280
bad guy charm to keep things moving along,
and never distracting too much from the movie's

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.350
various missions.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.540
Meanwhile, Joaquin Phoenix could've very well
stolen the show.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.320
That'd be great for the actor, but maybe not
so awesome for The Avengers, which needed

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.810
to have audiences already rooting for Captain
America by the time he first shows up on screen

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:34.810
in the modern age.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.440
"I gotta say, it's an honor to meet you, officially.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.180
I sort of met you.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.360
I watched you sleeping."

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.600
So while we could mourn the Red Skull we never
got, we might be better off getting excited

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.760
for the Joker we will have by the end of the
year.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.580
With just a few photos and one trailer, Joaquin
Phoenix's Joker, directed by Todd Phillips,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.280
has gotten tons of fans buzzing.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.880
Poke around on the internet for just a few
minutes, and you'll see no shortage of hot

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.710
takes saying that Phoenix's Joker has the
potential to outshine even Heath Ledger's

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.170
legendary turn as the villain.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.480
Wen, for his part, agrees.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.720
"The world of the Dark Knight has been established
as a much darker take on comic book storytelling

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.500
and again one that is perfect for both Batman
and the Joker.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.560
Phoenix is one of the few A-list actors that
I can see possibly living up to the iconic

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.400
Joker that Heath Ledger gave us."

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.080
Overall, Wen thinks the world is much better
off with Phoenix putting his stamp on the

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.140
Joker, explaining, "I think he's perfect for
the current Joker film that he's in.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.120
He's such a fantastic character actor."

